 "caveats": "Double-check before recording: (1) 'Four in ten' rounds 38.6%; the survey was online and these early memories are 'likely' fictional by the authors' judgement, not proven, so keep 'probably'. 'Averaged around age three' = mean 3.2 years. (2) Rovee-Collier's one-week figure is for 3-month-olds under her standard training; it shows the memory lasts days, not that babies keep it for life. (3) Yale 2025: only 26 infants, memory tested within the same scan session (exact delay not checked), effect strongest after 12 months; it shows encoding, not that the memories last. 'Playback seems to fail' is the authors' interpretation, hence 'seems'. Spelling 'recognise/centre' is British, as in earlier scripts. (4) Frankland neurogenesis and Guskjolen light-reactivation work are in mice; recovery was partial ('flickered'), and 'months later' rests on the ~90-day figure in secondary coverage; check the Current Biology paper if a reviewer has access. 'His team' = Frankland's lab (Axel Guskjolen lead author). (5) Simcock & Hayne: first author is Gabrielle Simcock; the line credits 'Harlene Hayne's lab', which is accurate but a reviewer may prefer naming both. (6) Freud deliberately omitted. No medical claims. v2 storyline: the library-renovation image is a metaphor for the neurogenesis idea (Akers et al. 2014, mice); 'flickered back' = partial recovery (Guskjolen et al. 2018, mice); the Yale line describes encoding activity that predicted later recognition (Yates et al. 2025, 26 infants)."
